        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>Psychosocial dynamics within the context of spaceflight have been shown to have a large impact on crew performance and well-being. The increased social support provided by crewmembers along with increased social interdependence has the potential to cause differential levels of interpersonal and/or intimate relationships among subsets of the crew. The valance of such relationships within a team must be viewed at both the dyadic level and at the team level. Given the potential for variation in the type and intensity of interpersonal relationships within the crew, this aspect of crew dynamics and its impact on the behavioral health and performance of the crew is a critical research gap.<br><br>Interpersonal relations within teams have been conceptualized to include (a) role-based behaviors, such as team members relating to others as role occupants, (b) close or friendship relations, and (c) intimate or romantic relations. Although positive interpersonal relations are critical to effective team functioning, variations in the strength and type of relationship ties can lead to breakdowns such as the formation of subgroups and cliques.<br><br>The primary product of this experiment will be a comprehensive and validated countermeasure designed to mitigate adverse effects of alterations in interpersonal relationships and sustain individual and team well-being in future long duration missions.<br><br>Specific aims of this multi-faceted program include:<br><ol><li><span style="background-color: white;">Determine the risks to team functioning and performance impacts resulting from development, maintenance, and breakdown of different types of interpersonal relationships (e.g., conflict) within a team along with key contributors of resilience to shifts in these team dynamics.</span></li><li>Develop, test, and validate an individual- and team- based training and intervention recommendations (and/or other countermeasures) to prevent adverse effects resulting from such shifts in team dynamics.<br><br></li></ol>APPROACH:<br>To achieve the specific aims outlined above, this experiment has been broken down into three studies.<br><br>Study 1 will examine the disparities in interpersonal relationships among crewmembers based on data obtained from prior analog missions relating to subgroup formation and its impact on crew wellbeing. The research team’s current database consists of 640 crew communication transcripts representing 188 mission days, 16 HERA missions (Campaign 1 [7-days]; Campaign 2 [14-days]; and Campaign 3 [30-days]), and 64 crewmembers. <br><br>This portion of the study will employ a unique and innovative approach to examine interpersonal dynamics among crew members by applying and adapting a linguistic analysis technique called Language Style Matching (LSM). This technique is an interactive assessment of verbal coordination that provides a measure of synchrony (or closeness) in social interaction by examining the extent to which two or more persons in conversation match each other’s speech style. This is accomplished by examining the relative use of nine function-word categories (e.g., personal pronouns, adverbs) that can be drawn from the current transcript data. By utilizing the validated LSM measure, as well as adapting this measure to examine affective-word categories (e.g., positive emotion, negative emotion, anger), the research team will create a proxy measure to evaluate closeness between crewmembers. <br><br>Study 2 will focus on factors that promote and inhibit subgroup formation within a laboratory setting. Prior research has suggested that whereas factors such as increased familiarity among two or more team members may led to subgroup formation, introducing a cross-cutting variable may inhibit subgroup formation. This creates a divergent strategy in which subgroups are broken up into other subgroups that do not align with the original subgrouping. An alternative is a convergent strategy in which the attempt is made to merge the subgroups into one larger group, thus helping to promote team-level interdependence. The research team will utilize a 3 x 2 between-subjects design using 4-person teams to represent the different strategies (divergent, convergent, control) and levels of familiarity within the team (high and low). In addition to measuring the effectiveness of these types of strategies, demographic data, personality measures, process measures, and outcome measures will also be collected.<br><br>Study 3 will also be conducted in a laboratory setting and will investigate the efficacy of two aspects of the Detect, Integrate and React (DIR) countermeasure. First, the PI team will examine the direct effectiveness of the DIR countermeasure on the core competencies they train (e.g., interpersonal sensitivity, perspective taking). Second, we want to examine the effectiveness of the training on team-level outcomes in a team-based setting. To accomplish this, the laboratory study will be carried out in three phases: Phase 1 (DIR Training), Phase 2 (DIR testing), and Phase 3 (Team-based Task). For data collection, researchers will recruit 4-person teams. Phases 1 and 2 will be completed at the individual level and Phase 3 will be completed collectively as a team. <br><br>RESULTS:<br>This research is currently ongoing, results will be made available at a later date.<br><br><br>
        </div>

